WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.690
Faster tokenization can help search and retrieval
systems.

2
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:11.400
Hugging Face shared new BERT optimizations on October
fifteenth.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:13.479
The work is still a draft pull request.

4
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.167
The main idea is to avoid unnecessary work.

5
00:00:17.267 --> 00:00:23.384
Skip eligible ASCII ranges, cache Unicode decisions,
and allocate output only when text changes.

6
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.413
A headline speedup is not an application speedup.

7
00:00:27.513 --> 00:00:30.141
Test your own languages and input sizes.

8
00:00:30.241 --> 00:00:33.479
Short strings and Chinese text need separate
attention.

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.723
Compatibility includes token identifiers and source
offsets.

10
00:00:37.823 --> 00:00:43.246
Two spellings of an accented word can produce matching
tokens, yet refer to different source positions.

11
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.666
Our baseline check found a useful trap.

12
00:00:46.766 --> 00:00:50.099
Chinese padding can change when normalization runs
twice.

13
00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:53.436
String equality alone is the wrong acceptance
condition.

14
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.549
Pin the tokenizer and vocabulary.

15
00:00:56.649 --> 00:00:58.777
Compare outputs before measuring speed.

16
00:00:58.877 --> 00:01:00.109
Keep a rollback path.

17
00:01:00.209 --> 00:01:03.627
Faster preprocessing matters when the full pipeline
stays correct.

